
<p class="wp-block-paragraph">The <a href="https://piksiboston.weebly.com/" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer noopener">Philosophy in an Inclusive Key Summer Institutes</a> (PIKSI) in Boston aim to &#8220;broaden inclusion within professional philosophy.&#8221; In part because such efforts are currently unpopular with the powers that be, its primary funding, from MIT and Harvard, has been cut.</p>



<span id="more-27161"></span>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">To cope with the cuts, its organizers say that &#8220;it is our plan to shrink it a bit and see what we can do to raise funds for 2027, with the hope that we can build it back up with other funding in 2028 and beyond.&#8221; </p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">In the following guest post, <a href="https://sallyhaslanger.weebly.com/" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer noopener">Sally Haslanger</a> (MIT) discusses what PIKSI Boston, which has been in operation since 2015, does, its impact on students, and how you can help with efforts to sustain the program.</p>



